# Systems of complex, high-performance and pressurized airplanes: PHAK chapters 7 and 17; AFH chapters 12
# and 13; AC 91-74B (icing); 14 CFR 91.211. ACS I.G, VIII.A, VIII.B, IX.C.
ITEMS = [
    # ---------- Practice Test 1 ----------
    S(1, 2, "Constant-speed propeller", "With a constant-speed propeller, how should you increase power for a climb?",
      ["Increase RPM first, then manifold pressure", "Increase manifold pressure first, then RPM", "Increase both at once with the throttle only"],
      "To avoid high manifold pressure at low RPM, which overstresses the engine, raise RPM with the propeller control first and then add manifold pressure. To reduce power, lower manifold pressure first, then RPM (PHAK chapter 7)."),
    S(1, 2, "Turbocharging", "What is the critical altitude of a turbocharged engine?",
      ["The highest altitude at which it can still hold rated manifold pressure", "The altitude at which the turbocharger first starts to work", "The altitude above which supplemental oxygen is required"],
      "A turbocharger can maintain sea-level manifold pressure up to its critical altitude. Above it the wastegate is fully closed and manifold pressure falls with altitude (PHAK chapter 7)."),
    S(1, 2, "Retractable gear", "What keeps the landing gear from retracting while the airplane is on the ground?",
      ["A squat switch on the gear that senses the weight of the airplane", "The gear warning horn, which locks the gear handle", "The constant-speed propeller governor"],
      "A safety (squat) switch on a main gear strut opens the retraction circuit while the strut is compressed by the airplane's weight (PHAK chapter 7)."),
    S(1, 2, "Fuel injection", "What problem is common when restarting a hot fuel-injected engine?",
      ["Vapor lock in the fuel lines makes it hard to start", "Carburetor ice blocks the fuel flow", "The engine runs too lean to idle once started"],
      "Heat soak after shutdown can vaporize fuel in the lines and injectors, making hot starts difficult. Fuel injection does not suffer carburetor ice (PHAK chapter 7)."),
    S(1, 2, "Pressurization", "In a pressurized airplane, what mainly controls the cabin altitude?",
      ["The outflow valve, which lets air out of the cabin at a controlled rate", "The safety valve, which opens at every altitude change", "The inflow of outside air through the cabin vents"],
      "Air is pumped into the cabin and the pressure controller sets cabin altitude by moving the outflow valve. The safety and dump valves are for overpressure and emergencies (PHAK chapter 7)."),
    S(1, 2, "Oxygen", "Why should you use aviator's breathing oxygen rather than industrial oxygen?",
      ["It has very little water vapor, which could freeze in the system at altitude", "It is mixed with nitrogen to prevent oxygen toxicity", "It is pressurized to a lower pressure for safety"],
      "Aviator's breathing oxygen is at least 99.5 percent pure with almost no water. Moisture in other grades can freeze in valves and lines at low temperatures (PHAK chapter 17)."),
    # ---------- Practice Test 2 ----------
    S(2, 2, "Manifold pressure", "With the engine stopped on the ramp, about what does the manifold pressure gauge read?",
      ["The local atmospheric pressure, near the altimeter setting at sea level", "Zero inches of mercury", "The maximum takeoff manifold pressure"],
      "With the engine off, the intake manifold is at outside air pressure, so the gauge shows about 29 to 30 inches at a sea-level airport, less at higher fields."),
    S(2, 2, "Feathering", "Why is the propeller of a failed engine feathered in a twin?",
      ["To turn the blades edge-on and cut the drag of a windmilling propeller", "To keep the failed engine turning so it can restart", "To increase the thrust of the operating engine"],
      "A windmilling propeller makes a lot of drag. Feathering aligns the blades with the airflow so the propeller stops and drag is greatly reduced, which also lowers Vmc (AFH chapter 13)."),
    S(2, 2, "Electrical system", "The ammeter shows a steady discharge and the low-voltage light is on. What should you do?",
      ["Shed nonessential electrical loads and land as soon as practical", "Turn off the master switch and continue the flight", "Increase RPM until the discharge stops"],
      "These signs point to an alternator or charging failure. The battery is now carrying the load, so turn off what you do not need and plan to land before it runs down (ACS IX.C)."),
    S(2, 2, "Deicing boots", "According to current FAA guidance for most airplanes, when should you operate pneumatic deicing boots?",
      ["As soon as icing begins, unless the manual says otherwise", "Only after a quarter inch of ice has built up, to avoid ice bridging", "Only on the ground before takeoff"],
      "AC 91-74B says ice bridging is not a practical concern for modern boots and that they should be activated at the first sign of ice, unless the airplane's manual directs otherwise."),
    S(2, 2, "Emergency gear extension", "The landing gear does not extend with the normal system. What should you use first?",
      ["The emergency extension procedure in the airplane's manual", "A series of hard landings to shake the gear loose", "Full power and a steep climb to free the gear"],
      "Run the checklist and use the airplane's emergency extension system, such as a hand crank, hand pump or free-fall release, as the manual describes (ACS IX.C)."),
    S(2, 2, "Cylinder head temperature", "The cylinder head temperature is near the red line during a long climb. What helps cool the engine?",
      ["Open the cowl flaps, enrich the mixture and lower the nose for more airspeed", "Close the cowl flaps and lean the mixture", "Raise the nose to climb out of the heat faster"],
      "More cooling air (cowl flaps open, higher airspeed), a richer mixture and less power all lower engine temperatures (PHAK chapter 7)."),
    # ---------- Drill only ----------
    S(0, 2, "Gear warning", "When does the landing gear warning horn sound in most retractable-gear airplanes?",
      ["When power is reduced below a set level with the gear not down and locked", "When airspeed rises above the gear extension speed", "Whenever the flaps are lowered with the gear up or down"],
      "The horn sounds when the throttle is pulled back below a set manifold pressure, or the flaps are lowered past a set point, while the gear is not down and locked (PHAK chapter 7)."),
    S(0, 2, "Pressurization", "An airplane flies at 22,000 feet with a cabin altitude of 8,000 feet. What is the cabin differential pressure?",
      ["The difference between cabin pressure and outside air pressure", "The pressure the outflow valve adds above sea level", "The difference between cabin altitude and flight altitude in feet"],
      "Differential pressure is the cabin pressure minus the outside pressure, in psi. The structure is built for a maximum differential, which limits how low the cabin altitude can be held (PHAK chapter 7)."),
    S(0, 2, "Oxygen systems", "Which oxygen system gives oxygen under pressure and is needed at very high altitudes, above about 40,000 feet?",
      ["Pressure-demand", "Continuous-flow", "Diluter-demand"],
      "Continuous-flow systems are common in light airplanes. Diluter-demand systems mix oxygen with cabin air on demand up to about 40,000 feet. Above that, oxygen must be delivered under positive pressure (PHAK chapter 17)."),
    S(0, 2, "Pulse oximeter", "What does a pulse oximeter tell you in flight?",
      ["The oxygen saturation of your blood", "The oxygen pressure left in the bottle", "The cabin altitude"],
      "A pulse oximeter on a finger shows blood oxygen saturation. It helps you notice hypoxia before symptoms are obvious (PHAK chapter 17)."),
    S(0, 2, "Propeller governor", "With the propeller control set, what keeps a constant-speed propeller at the same RPM as airspeed increases in a descent?",
      ["The governor increases blade angle", "The governor decreases blade angle", "The engine automatically reduces manifold pressure"],
      "If RPM starts to rise, the governor's flyweights move outward and the blades go to a higher pitch, adding load until RPM returns to the set value (PHAK chapter 7)."),
    S(0, 2, "Turbocharging", "What is an overboost in a turbocharged engine?",
      ["Manifold pressure above the engine's allowed limit", "RPM above the red line", "Turbine inlet temperature below normal"],
      "Overboost means manifold pressure exceeds the limit, which can damage the engine. Advance the throttle slowly, especially in cold dense air (PHAK chapter 7)."),
    S(0, 2, "Ice protection", "What is the difference between an anti-ice system and a deice system?",
      ["Anti-ice prevents ice from forming; deice removes ice after it forms", "Anti-ice removes ice; deice prevents it", "They are two names for the same equipment"],
      "Anti-ice equipment, such as heated pitot tubes or propeller heat, keeps ice from forming. Deice equipment, such as pneumatic boots, sheds ice that has already built up."),
    S(0, 2, "Fuel crossfeed", "In a light twin, what is fuel crossfeed used for?",
      ["Letting an engine draw fuel from the tanks on the other wing", "Moving fuel from one wing's tanks to the other in flight", "Feeding both engines from an auxiliary fuselage tank"],
      "Crossfeed lets an engine use fuel from the opposite wing's tanks, for example to extend range with one engine inoperative. It does not transfer fuel between tanks (AFH chapter 13)."),
    S(0, 2, "Glass cockpit", "On an airplane with electronic flight displays, which unit supplies attitude and heading information?",
      ["The attitude and heading reference system (AHRS)", "The air data computer (ADC)", "The engine monitoring system"],
      "The AHRS uses solid-state sensors to give pitch, roll and heading. The air data computer supplies airspeed, altitude and vertical speed (PHAK chapter 8)."),
    S(0, 2, "Hydraulic systems", "In many retractable-gear airplanes, what usually powers the gear?",
      ["An electrically driven hydraulic pump or an electric motor", "Air from the pressurization system", "The vacuum pump that drives the gyros"],
      "Light retractable-gear airplanes typically use an electrically driven hydraulic pump or an electric motor and gearbox to raise and lower the gear (PHAK chapter 7)."),
    S(0, 2, "Pressurization", "What does the cabin rate-of-climb indicator in a pressurized airplane show?",
      ["How fast the cabin altitude is changing", "How fast the airplane is climbing", "How fast the outflow valve is opening"],
      "The cabin rate of climb shows the change in cabin altitude, normally kept gentle for passenger comfort, separate from the airplane's own vertical speed."),
]
